WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.916 --> 00:00:05.579
The first thing I had to do at school was get permission to use my new invention.

2
00:00:05.599 --> 00:00:06.660
Luckily, Mrs.

3
00:00:06.680 --> 00:00:09.062
Romano thought it sounded like a great idea.

4
00:00:09.142 --> 00:00:09.603
So did Mr.

5
00:00:09.643 --> 00:00:34.903
Dalmati, the recess At recess all the kids came running out and got in line for the slide.

6
00:00:36.444 --> 00:00:39.926
Josh, Molly, and I ran over with the two shopping bags.

7
00:00:39.946 --> 00:00:44.029
"Pick a number for the slide!"

8
00:00:44.409 --> 00:00:45.169
called Josh.

9
00:00:45.189 --> 00:00:47.711
"Then grab a toy!"

10
00:00:47.731 --> 00:00:50.673
"What are the numbers for?"

11
00:00:51.133 --> 00:00:52.214
asked one little girl.

12
00:00:52.234 --> 00:00:56.777
"It's to hold your place in line for the slide," I explained.

13
00:00:57.958 --> 00:01:00.620
"You can take a toy too," Molly added.

14
00:01:01.644 --> 00:01:04.066
"When your number is called, turn in your toy.

15
00:01:04.386 --> 00:01:06.528
Then you don't have to wait in line for the slide."

16
00:01:06.548 --> 00:01:10.091
"Cool!" said a fifth-grade boy.

17
00:01:10.111 --> 00:01:12.033
"That sounds like fun!"

18
00:01:14.114 --> 00:01:17.137
Josh handed out the numbers, and I gave out the toys.

19
00:01:18.718 --> 00:01:21.761
Pretty soon we were out of numbers and out of toys.

20
00:01:21.781 --> 00:01:26.404
"Numbers one, two, and three, line up!"

21
00:01:26.825 --> 00:01:27.545
called Josh.

22
00:01:28.986 --> 00:01:30.548
Three kids ran over to us.

23
00:01:31.904 --> 00:01:34.145
They handed in their numbers along with their toys.

24
00:01:35.106 --> 00:01:36.727
Then they climbed up to the slide.

25
00:01:38.607 --> 00:01:41.549
You would not believe how great my invention worked!

26
00:01:42.569 --> 00:01:43.670
All the kids loved it.

27
00:01:45.111 --> 00:01:48.332
They traded toys and ran around, laughing and playing.

28
00:01:49.793 --> 00:01:52.434
Everyone stopped pushing and shoving in line.

29
00:01:53.595 --> 00:01:55.356
In fact, there was no line.

30
00:01:56.544 --> 00:02:00.865
We only called out three numbers at a time, so kids got to go on the slide without

31
00:02:00.925 --> 00:02:02.226
waiting for very long.

32
00:02:04.486 --> 00:02:08.147
I felt proud to see my Pick-a-Number invention working so well.

33
00:02:10.228 --> 00:02:12.488
Mr. Dalmati was happy too.

34
00:02:12.508 --> 00:02:15.949
"Great idea, Iggy," he told me.

35
00:02:15.969 --> 00:02:19.090
"You solved a big recess problem."

36
00:02:20.170 --> 00:02:21.971
A first-grade girl ran over to me.

37
00:02:21.991 --> 00:02:25.892
"This number game is so much fun!"

38
00:02:26.468 --> 00:02:27.008
she said.

39
00:02:27.028 --> 00:02:30.029
"Can we play it again tomorrow?"

40
00:02:30.049 --> 00:02:35.170
"We can play it whenever we have outdoor recess," I said.

41
00:02:36.171 --> 00:02:40.352
The kids loved the number game almost as much as they loved the slide!

42
00:02:41.712 --> 00:02:44.833
After recess I wrote about my Pick-a-Number invention for Mr.

43
00:02:44.893 --> 00:02:49.114
Bright. Then I handed my paper to Mrs.

44
00:02:49.194 --> 00:02:52.155
Romano. She put it with the other ideas.

45
00:02:53.275 --> 00:02:55.756
Everything was working out just the way I had hoped.

46
00:02:57.728 --> 00:03:01.170
But there was one thing that still bothered me—Jenny.

47
00:03:02.911 --> 00:03:05.012
She didn't take a turn calling out numbers.

48
00:03:06.053 --> 00:03:07.354
She said it wasn't any fun.

49
00:03:08.655 --> 00:03:12.617
Not only that, but she got a "D" on her math test that day.

50
00:03:13.998 --> 00:03:16.299
She told me about it on the way home from school.

51
00:03:16.319 --> 00:03:22.122
"I studied really hard," she said with tears in her eyes.

52
00:03:22.142 --> 00:03:24.264
"I just don't know what's wrong!

53
00:03:24.992 --> 00:03:27.573
My dad is going to be so upset."

54
00:03:28.773 --> 00:03:30.894
If only I could think of a way to help her!

55
00:03:30.914 --> 00:03:36.175
"Sara is having trouble in school too," Jenny blurted out.

56
00:03:37.676 --> 00:03:38.476
She sighed.

57
00:03:38.496 --> 00:03:42.437
"At least I'm not the only one."

58
00:03:43.718 --> 00:03:44.298
I looked at her.

59
00:03:44.318 --> 00:03:45.998
"How do you know that?"

60
00:03:46.298 --> 00:03:46.798
I asked.

61
00:03:47.999 --> 00:03:51.860
Sara was Jenny's friend, the one who had just moved to another neighborhood.

62
00:03:53.076 --> 00:03:56.558
"I talked to Sara last night on the phone," Jenny explained.

63
00:03:56.578 --> 00:04:00.019
"She told me she failed a reading test.

64
00:04:00.439 --> 00:04:03.100
It was her first test at her new school.

65
00:04:03.801 --> 00:04:06.282
She's worried everyone will think she's dumb."

66
00:04:08.763 --> 00:04:12.944
That night, when I went to bed, I was still thinking about Jenny's problem.

67
00:04:14.405 --> 00:04:16.866
I lay there, looking up through the skylight.

68
00:04:17.947 --> 00:04:20.908
Sometimes, if it's a clear night, I can see the stars.

69
00:04:22.108 --> 00:04:24.851
I imagine that they are winking just for me, Iggy.

70
00:04:26.033 --> 00:04:29.697
I pretend they're my friends, helping me to think of bright ideas.

71
00:04:30.878 --> 00:04:34.222
That night the stars looked clear and sharp.

72
00:04:35.243 --> 00:04:38.687
They winked, as though they were trying to tell me something.

73
00:04:39.769 --> 00:04:42.331
But what were they trying to tell me?
